WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.490
Hello there, I'm Gav.

00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:02.323
I'm Dan.

00:00:02.323 --> 00:00:05.220
We're The Slow Mo Guys,
without our lab coats.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.790
Yeah, we've actually got
these very fashionable

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:08.320
sort of space suit things.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.602
Little bit Babylon Zoo.

00:00:09.602 --> 00:00:11.268
They are.

00:00:11.268 --> 00:00:12.550
I feel like I should be playing
like a robot the whole time.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:13.540
Yeah.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.100
Where are we right now?

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.040
We're at the metal casting research lab

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.350
at Purdue University.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.280
And we're going to be doing
some molten metal pouring

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.490
onto this plate.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:23.323
I'll stop you there.

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:24.700
Not just any metal, right.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.330
I've seen, you know, videos of
molten aluminum being poured

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.090
but that melts at 600
degrees C and we're doing

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:31.923
Child's play.

00:00:31.923 --> 00:00:32.885
Nothing isn't it, nothing.

00:00:32.885 --> 00:00:33.863
You can do that in the back garden.

00:00:33.863 --> 00:00:37.080
This is copper melted over 1,085 degrees

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:37.913
and it's going to be pouring

00:00:37.913 --> 00:00:40.280
at 1,200 degrees C.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.860
Over 2,00 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.570
I think for this one resolution is going

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.130
to make this a lot nicer than speed

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.680
so we'll film it at 1,000 frames a second

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.430
but in 4K.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:50.330
First we'll get a light on it

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.720
and I think after that we'll just let it

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.380
we'll expose it to its own glow

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:55.370
so it'll just be, you know,

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:58.160
surreal molten droplets splashing in 4K.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.003
I think it'll be class.

00:00:59.003 --> 00:00:59.850
I think it'll be class too.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:00.860
Yeah.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:01.730
Well now that we're agreed

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.680
on the fact that it'll
be class, let's move on.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.763
They're melted by induction,

00:01:06.671 --> 00:01:07.653
so the coil,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.230
electrical coil in the
refractory there that's

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.247
creating a 10,000 HTZ

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.040
oscillating signal that's

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.160
creating Eddy currents in the metal.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:18.993
Yeah.

00:01:18.993 --> 00:01:20.580
And resistibly heating that and melting it

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.253
so there's no gas that's burned.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.036
It's a very clean way
to melt things though.

00:01:26.036 --> 00:01:27.480
Having a bit of an issue here

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.520
What's up?

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.380
Basically, I'm pretty scared.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.213
Yeah.

00:01:31.213 --> 00:01:33.310
Obviously 1200 degrees hot melting copper

00:01:35.217 --> 00:01:37.850
and I've got these to port with

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:41.030
and I'm like trying to practice with it

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.580
and by the time I've
like got the grip right

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:43.890
I'm like okay yeah I'm ready

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.080
it's already solidified.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.360
You've got to pour it in like

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.760
Yeah it's heavy.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.370
Cause it's a thing full of metal

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.390
and I've got to grab it
with this and pour it, yeah.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:53.550
Yeah.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.310
It's not easy cause it's
1200 degrees at the minute

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:56.680
but then as soon as it goes below sort

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.397
of, what is it, 1085 then it solidifies

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.400
so you've got very few,
very small amount of time

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.540
to be able to pour it.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:06.610
You always forget that about metal

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:08.613
is that when it's liquid it's still metal.

00:02:08.613 --> 00:02:10.592
Yeah, it's still heavy.

00:02:10.592 --> 00:02:11.675
Here we go.

00:02:13.377 --> 00:02:14.462
You want to be up higher, right?

00:02:14.462 --> 00:02:15.996
Yep.

00:02:15.996 --> 00:02:17.261
Get it in the center too.

00:02:17.261 --> 00:02:18.821
Okay.

00:02:18.821 --> 00:02:19.654
Ready?

00:02:26.358 --> 00:02:27.191
Cool. Got it?

00:02:27.191 --> 00:02:28.024
Yep.

00:02:30.603 --> 00:02:32.103
Did you get hit?

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:34.433
You alright?

00:02:34.433 --> 00:02:35.622
Awesome.

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:36.455
Did you get it?

00:02:36.455 --> 00:02:38.121
Yeah.

00:02:38.121 --> 00:02:38.954
You got my hands.

00:02:38.954 --> 00:02:40.257
Really? No.

00:02:40.257 --> 00:02:41.160
You alright?

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.030
Yeah.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.183
You can't really push the
button with these things.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:14.993
How did that go?

00:03:14.993 --> 00:03:15.910
It went well.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:16.890
I didn't chicken out.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:17.723
It was good aim.

00:03:17.723 --> 00:03:20.042
You went really high and it
landed straight on the pan.

00:03:20.042 --> 00:03:20.875
I was quite happy with it.

00:03:20.875 --> 00:03:22.680
It's quite distracting
and a lot of pressure

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.907
when you've got molten copper
at 1200 degrees in your hand

00:03:25.907 --> 00:03:27.437
and you like trying to aim it.

00:03:27.437 --> 00:03:29.270
Why don't we show off our little splat

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.103
that we've got.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:32.663
That went nice.

00:03:32.663 --> 00:03:34.260
I think what we'll do now
for a bit of variation

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.420
is repeat the experiment we'll
turn off the lights though

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.800
and I'm going to go wide open on this lens

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:43.423
so hopefully we'll just be
illuminated by its red hotness.

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:52.563
Whoo!

00:03:54.452 --> 00:03:55.285
Are you alright?

00:03:55.285 --> 00:03:56.168
Yeah.

00:03:56.168 --> 00:03:58.085
It's gone everywhere.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.200
He did well to get it in focus actually.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.030
The focus that I gave you is about an inch

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:44.930
and you are nailing it.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:45.763
That's colly.

00:04:45.763 --> 00:04:46.596
I love that.

00:04:46.596 --> 00:04:47.429
This is one of those things

00:04:47.429 --> 00:04:48.500
that I could just watch this all day.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.500
This could be a screensaver for me.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:51.490
That looked deluxe.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:54.330
It looked like melted metal rain.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.730
Perfect balls of glowing hot metal falling

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:57.563
and then just splashing.

00:04:57.563 --> 00:04:59.460
This is exact same as
water where it just turns

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.260
into individual droplets
by the time it hits.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:03.632
You get loads of lovely splashes.

00:05:03.632 --> 00:05:04.820
Very surreal looking.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.040
Yeah except rain doesn't
glow and spread everywhere

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.550
and burn people.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:09.383
You got me.

00:05:09.383 --> 00:05:10.451
You got me in the hand.

00:05:10.451 --> 00:05:11.321
I was actually aiming to you.

00:05:11.321 --> 00:05:14.150
I was like that wasn't
high enough last time.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:15.070
Yeah keep going, keep going.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.860
Thankfully we didn't crack a lens

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.730
and just some skin got burned on my end.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.720
So actually once again
the glowing hot metal

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.040
where I get burned where I
get burned and you don't.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.760
I don't know how that keeps happening

00:05:27.886 --> 00:05:28.719
Ow!

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.240
I was standing next to it pouring it

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.150
but to be fair when I was watching

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.330
because you were watching the
camera I was pouring it here

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.260
and it spread, you know, a good couple

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.360
of meters everywhere
like liquid metal pouring

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.193
all over the place.

00:05:42.193 --> 00:05:43.060
You can probably find balls of it.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.400
Yeah, well hopefully
you enjoyed that video.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:46.233
Please let us know

00:05:46.233 --> 00:05:47.700
if you liked these science lab experiments

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.550
cause we can do more.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.220
Feel free to follow us on
Twitter, buy some merch,

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:53.070
and if anyone knows

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:56.170
how to power down Dan please let me know.

00:05:56.170 --> 00:05:57.303
He's out of control.

